WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.788
NIST PQC standards use parameter sets and security categories to describe intended

00:00:04.788 --> 00:00:06.741
strength levels and operational choices.

00:00:06.741 --> 00:00:08.304
At a high level,

00:00:08.304 --> 00:00:12.991
a security category helps compare an algorithm parameter set to a target

00:00:12.991 --> 00:00:14.945
level of classical security strength.

00:00:14.945 --> 00:00:18.070
Practitioners do not need to perform the mathematics,

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:24.320
but they do need to understand that parameter choices affect both security posture and system behavior.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.008
Parameter sets influence key sizes, ciphertext sizes, signature sizes, performance, memory use,

00:00:29.008 --> 00:00:30.571
bandwidth, and protocol overhead.

00:00:30.571 --> 00:00:34.477
Larger or stronger is not automatically better for every deployment.

00:00:34.477 --> 00:00:37.993
A high-volume service, a constrained device, a certificate-heavy workflow,

00:00:37.993 --> 00:00:41.899
and an offline signing process may all have different limits.

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:46.587
A parameter choice that is acceptable in one environment may cause interoperability

00:00:46.587 --> 00:00:48.541
or performance problems in another.

00:00:48.541 --> 00:00:53.619
This is why PQC migration requires testing with real protocols and realistic traffic.

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:56.744
The impact is not only the algorithm benchmark.

00:00:56.744 --> 00:01:01.432
It is the behavior of the whole system: handshake size, packet fragmentation,

00:01:01.432 --> 00:01:05.729
certificate chain size, firmware image size, hardware capacity, latency, memory pressure,

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:07.682
logging, monitoring, and failure handling.

00:01:07.682 --> 00:01:10.417
Small assumptions can become large deployment issues.

00:01:10.417 --> 00:01:15.495
The practical approach is to match parameters to risk, protocol, and operational constraints.

00:01:15.495 --> 00:01:20.574
Start with standards-based options, then test in the environments where they will run.

00:01:20.574 --> 00:01:24.480
Security teams should bring architects, developers, infrastructure owners, device teams,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.824
and vendors into the conversation early.

00:01:26.824 --> 00:01:30.340
PQC is cryptographic, but its tradeoffs are deeply operational.
